\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Left: estimated school quality $\theta _k$ (school fixed effect from the log-odds regression, 2021--22 through 2024--25), all 71{} K programs ranked by quality, by TK-promotion eligibility (orange triangles: eligible; blue circles: ineligible). Horizontal lines mark the expected utility of lottery participation: dashed, the baseline formula with observed admission probabilities ($\mathrm  {EU} = \ensuremath  {-}1.48$); dotted, the capacity-constrained simulation ($\mathrm  {EU}^{\text  {cap}} = \ensuremath  {-}2.29$). Schools above a line are worth more as a certain seat than the lottery is in expectation. Promotion-eligible schools are spread across the quality distribution rather than concentrated at either end, though they are somewhat overrepresented in the lowest tail, consistent with the (informal) claim that TK placements skew toward under-demanded schools. Right: forgone option value $\mathrm  {EU} - \theta _k$ (\S  \ref {sec:cost-of-application}) for promotion-eligible schools, under each version of $\mathrm  {EU}$.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:quality-by-promotion-status}{{2}{7}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Kindergarten applications per school, by TK-promotion eligibility, for the 70{} schools observed in every cycle from 2021--22 to 2026--27 (Mission Education Center ES, absent after 2024--25, is excluded). Counts are observed requests, without adding promoted families back. Citywide, lottery applicants fell from 3{,}792{} in 2024--25 to 3{,}380{} in 2025--26, while 601{} families accepted promotion; ranked requests fell from 21{,}951{} to 18{,}681. Source: \texttt  {output/application\_effects\_summary.csv}.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:application-summary}{{1}{8}{}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Summary statistics.}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
